      "inputTokens": 168,
      "outputTokens": 1271,
      "costUsd": 0.011523,
      "pronunciationText": "Last video ended here, with this density. Where is X most likely to be, and where's the middle, with half the probability on each side? For this symmetric hump, both answers are one, right in the centre. Now a cookie, four centimetres in radius, with its choc chips spread evenly. In the ideal model, a chip lands at a random point on the cookie. Let X be its distance from the centre. Its density is x over eight, from zero to four. It climbs the whole way, because the outer rings hold more cookie. So distances near the edge are the most likely, and the density peaks at four. Yet half the probability lies within about two point eight three centimetres of the centre. So the most likely value and the middle can be far apart. How do we find each one?"
    },
    {
      "id": "scene-02",
      "changed": true,
      "audio": "public/audio/math-a12-s06/scene-02.wav",
      "durationInFrames": 1035,
      "worstWordGap": {
